---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Meter from "../components/Meter.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";
---

<Base title="How to read this site" description="What the labels on each entry mean and how much weight to give them.">
  <article class="wrap article">
    <a class="back" href="/library"
      ><svg class="arrow" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8h10M9 4l4 4-4 4"></path></svg> library</a
    >
    <h1 class="guide-title">how to read this site</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      A single study rarely settles a question. The labels on each entry are there to help you judge how much weight a
      finding deserves.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Three kinds of entry</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Study summary:</strong> a published paper, explained in plain language, with a link to the original.</li>
        <li><strong>Original research:</strong> experiments and analyses done for {site.name}. These have not been peer reviewed.</li>
        <li><strong>Note:</strong> a shorter piece, such as an idea, an explanation of a concept, or a reading list.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The evidence meter</h2>
      <p>
        Study summaries show the design of the study and a four-step meter. The meter reflects the design only. It is a
        rough guide to how much one study of that kind can tell us, not a score for how well the study was run. A careful
        observational study can be more reliable than a small or badly run trial.
      </p>
      <ul class="guide-levels">
        <li>
          <Meter level={4} label="pooled evidence" />
          <span>Meta-analyses and systematic reviews combine many studies. They are usually the strongest single source, but only as good as the studies they include.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Meter level={3} label="randomized" />
          <span>Randomized trials assign people to a treatment or a comparison by chance. This is the best design for showing that one thing causes another.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Meter level={2} label="observational" />
          <span>Observational studies follow what people already do. They can show that two things go together, but not reliably that one causes the other.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <Meter level={1} label="early evidence" />
          <span>Animal studies, cell studies, and case reports. Useful for generating ideas; results often do not carry over to people.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <p>
        Results are given in absolute terms wherever the paper allows, because a relative figure alone can make an effect
        look larger than it is. If a risk falls from 2 in 100 people to 1 in 100, that is a 50% relative reduction, but
        only 1 person in 100 is better off.
      </p>

      <h2>Participants and funding</h2>
      <p>
        Larger studies give steadier results, and findings in one group of people may not apply to another. Each summary
        also says who paid for the study when the paper reports it, since the source of funding can influence how results
        are framed.
      </p>

      <h2>Limits, dates, and corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Every summary can list what the study does not show. The "last reviewed" date says when the entry was last
        checked. If a paper is later corrected or retracted, the entry is marked so the change is visible.
      </p>

      <h2>Not medical advice</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing here is medical advice. The entries describe what studies found; they are not recommendations for any
        individual. Talk to a qualified professional before acting on health information.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
